PLATFORM NOTES
Wednesday, February 25, 2026

Vertical software's second act

The first act of vertical software, roughly 2010 to 2020, was about replacing paper and spreadsheets with a system of record for one industry: dental practices, restaurants, construction sites, auto dealers. The winners built a database shaped like the industry's workflow, sold it to owners who had never bought software before, and charged a subscription that looked expensive per seat and cheap per business.

The second act, underway now, is different in kind. The system of record exists; the question is what you can do with the data in it. And the answer, increasingly, is: run the business. Scheduling, pricing, ordering, staffing, the decisions the owner used to make on instinct, are being made or at least proposed by software that has seen a thousand businesses like theirs.

This changes the buyer. The first-act buyer was the owner, who wanted less paperwork. The second-act buyer is the operator, who wants a better Tuesday. It changes the pricing, from seats to outcomes. And it changes the competitive dynamics, because the incumbent system of record has the data but usually not the talent to do anything with it, and the startup has the talent but has to get the data out of the incumbent.

The pattern to watch is the connector war: incumbents locking down APIs, startups building around them, and the industry's customers stuck in the middle asking why their two vendors cannot talk to each other. It played out in accounting a decade ago and it is playing out in manufacturing and healthcare now. The incumbents won the first round of that war in accounting. I do not think they win it this time.

